+  - **At any time** (DEC-1164 (15)): the "never" list below protects running work.
   - **Never:** anything modified in the last 24 hours, anything a running process holds open (`lsof`), anything cited as evidence, the live session's own folder or the live daemon's folder, `~/.cache`, a ticket in progress's `~/gov-os-runs/` folder, the record worktree, the owner's EXAMIN copy, or the workbench.
   - **It lists** what it removed and what it skipped, and why. The figures before and after go in the daily digest.
-- **Closes (DEC-780, DEC-867; DEC-883, DEC-884).** Until W2-66 lands, the queue runs as today, one close at a time; the re-pin waits for DEC-884's ladder. A close runs in a quiet window. First write a fresh `gov checkpoint --ticket <id>` record for the ticket being closed (the close refuses a stale one: `CHECKPOINT_STALE`) and your own checkpoint, then rebuild immediately before the close, with no commit between (a commit makes the store stale).
-- **The pre-close gate (owner, standing, DEC-868), until W2-07 builds it into `gov close`:** before every close, after the rebuild, and start the close only if all of them pass (until the first re-pin, no close starts unless gates (a) to (h) all predict a pass, DEC-896 item 4): (a) `pytest --collect-only -q` over every suite the close will run, in one process, as the close runs them (`env -u PYTEST_ADDOPTS -u PYTEST_PLUGINS PYTHONPATH=<root>/src python3 -m pytest tests --collect-only -q -p no:cacheprovider --no-header`): no collection error; (b) `gov check`, with no commit since the rebuild: exit 0; (c) the ticket's checkpoint fresh; (d) the working tree clean, nothing modified, staged or untracked, as the close reads it (`tools/tree_diff.py`); (e) every commit of the ticket carries its trailers, as the close reads them, and every `Implements:` item is a decision id the register holds (or a Contract id the store holds), so a wrong `Implements:` is caught within the ticket, not at close time (`tools/trailer_gate.py`; owner, standing, DEC-927 item 2); (f) the suites that read the records green; (g) the live tests' allowlisted hosts reachable (DEC-892): if not, the queue holds and retries later, rather than spend a close on a network failure; (h) the work-without-a-task gate, run with the pinned judge's own code (`tools/unmeasured_gate.py`, the pin's python `-E`): no unmeasured commit in the ticket's range (DEC-896) (an untracked file refuses the close as `TREE_NOT_COMMITTED`, as W2-54's of 2026-10-20 showed). A failure stops the queue before the close, with its cause. `tools/close_queue.sh <id>[:<DEC-n>] …` does this (`:<DEC-n>` passes the owner's lift of an escalation); it exits non-zero after a refused close (exit 4, `CLOSE REFUSED` in its log; owner, standing, DEC-929 item 3). **While a queue runs (owner, standing, DEC-929 item 2), until the path map's lists move to record files of their own:** non-urgent record commits wait and go in between queues, since a path-map, prompt or brief change widens the next close to the full suite. **The secrets check's chance red (owner, DEC-897), until W2-69 is pinned:** when gate (b) is red only on the secrets-indexing check, you may re-run that close's queue once, and only after proving that the hit lies inside a longer identifier and in no tracked file (rule id, length, the enclosing word's length, the tracked-file count, and gitleaks clean on the whole line but red on the line cut at the match; positions and classes only, never the bytes). Record that evidence in the queue's log and the report. At most one re-run per close; any other red, or a second red, stops the queue until W2-69 is pinned. Never rebuild-until-green otherwise, and never weaken the check. **The interim skip guard (owner, DEC-899 item 7), until W2-57 lands:** after each `gov close`, the queue reads the close's own evidence block (its skips, each with test and reason) and stops before the close's outputs are committed, as a P1 package, on W1-38's "rulesync is not installed" skip whenever `rulesync --version` answers, and on any skip whose (test, reason) is not on the known list (`tools/skip_known.json`, versioned on the record branch; every addition named in the digest) (owner, DEC-915 items 1 and 2, a stopgap), so a silent rulesync skip (lens I's I4 F2) is never committed as a clean close.
+- **Closes (DEC-953, DEC-989, DEC-1132 (1)).** A ticket merges and closes in one step through the merge queue (`tools/land.py`), or closes by DEC-953's practice (`tk close` and a close record). The close windows, the per-close checkpoint and rebuild, the pre-close gate (a) to (h), `close_queue.sh`'s gates (the secrets check's chance red, DEC-897, and the interim skip guard, DEC-899 item 7, among them) and the record-commit wait while a queue runs (DEC-929 item 2) are lifted on this repository (DECISIONS-IN-FORCE, "Lifted"); they stay built and tested in the product.
+- **The pre-close gate (DEC-868):** lifted on this repository with the close windows (DEC-953; DECISIONS-IN-FORCE, "Lifted"); built into `gov close` in the product (W2-07).
 - **Your own record edits (owner, standing, DEC-868), until the hooks are back on (W2-08):** after editing the register, the plan, the path map, tickets or briefs, run the governance checks those files feed before you commit them: at least the path-map, schema and context checks (`tools/record_checks.sh`: `core-pathmap`, `core-schema`, `core-mutation`, `core-authority`, `core-graph`, `context-reproducibility`, each as the runner runs it), the suites that read the records, and **the placeholder scan (owner, standing, DEC-893):** no unfilled time or date (`00:2x`, `16:xx`), `TBD`, `TODO`, `XXX`, `xx` or `???`, and no `<…>` slot in a path-map line or patch, in any prepared entry, patch or record before it reaches the owner or a commit (`tools/placeholder_scan.py`; run it on a package or entry before sending it). Write exact times, never "HH:Mx". A red one is fixed before the commit. Run the close once; a second run without a change is a retry of the ratchet, which is forbidden. A refused close's repair ticket goes to `log/repair-tickets/` in your scratch. `accepted_reds` and `load_sensitive` only shrink, except by an owner package (DEC-766). <!-- placeholder-examples -->
 - **Row W2-L423's rule (owner, standing, DEC-911).** Under `.gov-runtime/`, commit hashes are written as 12 digits in every record, brief, return and log, and none of the rule's keywords is written there (call it "row W2-L423's rule"); put this in every brief. Keep the keywords and full hashes apart in tracked records too, and write a keyword only in parts in a tool or test. `tools/keyword_warn.py` lists, by path only, every file sessions write under `.gov-runtime/` that holds a keyword (not the index stores `codeintel/`, `store.db*` and `query-vector-cache.db*`, which the pinned check reads itself; owner, DEC-923): `record_checks.sh` prints it, and the close queue runs it after each rebuild and stops before the close on a hit. Gate (b) failing closed is the backstop. Never scope scratch out of the check, and never disable or allowlist the rule; W2-71 brings the only exemption.
 - **Commits.** Copies of assessment or audit reports (`docs/assessment/`, `docs/audit/` and the like) go in commits of their own, never mixed with ticket-file, register or plan edits (owner, standing, DEC-924 item 2). `--trailer` for Task, Role: orchestrator and Co-Authored-By (Implements: optional, R-12); commit with `-- <paths>` only; merges `--no-ff`, and the merge's tree equals `git merge-tree --write-tree HEAD^1 HEAD^2`. Never merge while anything is staged; under DEC-861 nothing stays staged.
